```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router16
diagnosis_text:
The fault is located entirely on router16, not on mswitch0 as the team suspected.
mswitch0's OSPF process is running correctly, has all four Campus 1 networks
(10.2.2.0/24, 172.16.10.0/24, 172.16.20.0/24, 172.16.30.0/24) configured in
area 0, and is actively waiting for a neighbor. The problem is that router16 —
the L3 relay device sitting between mswitch0 and router7 on the path to the
backbone — has its ospfd process completely dead. The frr.service on router16 is
in a "failed" state, ospfd does not appear in the process table (only zebra,
mgmtd, and staticd survive), and ospfd.conf is empty. Because ospfd is absent,
router16 participates in no OSPF adjacencies: it neither peers with mswitch0
over the 10.2.2.0/24 bond link (router16.bond0 = 10.2.2.1) nor with router7
over the 172.30.1.0/24 link (router16.eth2 = 172.30.1.1). Consequently,
mswitch0 forms zero OSPF neighbors and learns no external routes — its table
contains only directly connected subnets. Campus 1 PCs reach their local VLAN
gateway on mswitch0, but any packet destined beyond the campus is dropped there
because mswitch0 has no route to Campus 2 or backbone prefixes. router7
similarly has no OSPF neighbor on eth4 and never learns the Campus 1 VLAN
prefixes. The root cause is that the ospfd daemon on router16 was killed and
watchfrr was unable to restore it, severing the OSPF relay between mswitch0 and
router7. The corrective fix is to restore ospfd on router16: add proper OSPF
network statements (network 10.2.2.0/24 area 0 and network 172.30.1.0/24 area
0) to ospfd.conf, then restart the FRR service on router16 so ospfd starts and
adjacencies form with both mswitch0 and router7.
=== END ===
```